//? Title: Dirichlet probabilities and overdispersed counts
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Let category probabilities vary between batches

Each batch contains 20 items classified into three categories. The
Dirichlet shapes 0.5, 1.5 and 3 give mean category probabilities 0.1,
0.3 and 0.6. The Dirichlet–multinomial draws a fresh probability vector
for each batch and allocates that batch's items using those probabilities.

For the first category, the compound model predicts a count variance of
7.5. With a fixed probability of 0.1, the binomial variance would be 1.8.
The seeded simulation estimates the larger variance from repeated batches.

```{cpp, id=dirichlet-batch-counts, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/random/simplex.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::random;
    const std::vector<double> shapes{0.5, 1.5, 3};
    SplitMix64 rng(2026);
    const auto probabilities = dirichlet_sample(rng, shapes);
    std::println("One batch's probabilities are {:.3f}, {:.3f} and {:.3f}. "
                 "Their log density is {:.3f}.",
                 probabilities[0], probabilities[1], probabilities[2],
                 dirichlet_log_pdf(probabilities, shapes));
    std::println("");
    std::println("The probability of counts (2, 6, 12) is {:.4f}.",
                 std::exp(dirichlet_multinomial_log_pmf({2, 6, 12}, shapes)));

    constexpr int batches = 12000;
    double total = 0, squares = 0;
    for (int batch = 0; batch < batches; ++batch) {
        const double first = dirichlet_multinomial_sample(rng, 20, shapes).front();
        total += first;
        squares += first * first;
    }
    const double mean = total / batches;
    std::println("");
    std::println("The first-category count averages {:.2f} with variance {:.2f}.",
                 mean, squares / batches - mean * mean);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
One batch's probabilities are 0.018, 0.232 and 0.750. Their log density is 2.733.

The probability of counts (2, 6, 12) is 0.0094.

The first-category count averages 1.99 with variance 7.45.
// result_end

